4. Why MFA alone doesn't stop it

Traditional MFA (SMS codes, authenticator-app codes, push approvals) proves that **the person typing is you**, but it doesn't prove **which website they are typing into**. In an AiTM attack the victim completes MFA correctly, just on the wrong site, and the relay makes that look valid to the real service.

The attacker also never needs to guess or crack anything. They use the legitimate, freshly issued session.

Important: This does not mean MFA is useless. MFA still blocks the vast majority of simple password-theft attacks. AiTM is a more advanced technique, and the fix is *stronger* MFA, not abandoning MFA.

5. Real-world context
AiTM is not theoretical. Microsoft Threat Intelligence publicly reported in 2022 a large-scale AiTM phishing campaign that targeted over 10,000 organizations, using session-cookie theft to follow up with business email compromise attempts. Reverse-proxy phishing toolkits are publicly available, which lowers the skill needed to run these attacks, and security vendors continue to report this technique as a common way of bypassing standard MFA.
  
6. How defenders can reduce the risk

| Defense | How it helps |

| Phishing-resistant MFA (FIDO2 security keys, passkeys, Windows Hello for Business) | Authentication is bound to the real website's domain, so it fails on a look-alike proxy page. This is the strongest control. |
| Conditional Access policies | Require a compliant or managed device, block risky sign-ins, and restrict access by location or conditions. A stolen cookie replayed from an attacker's machine may fail these checks. |
| Token protection / token binding | Ties a session token to the device it was issued to, so a copied token is much less useful elsewhere. |
| Shorter session lifetimes and continuous access evaluation | Reduces how long a stolen session stays valuable. |
| User awareness | Teach people to check the address bar, be wary of unexpected login prompts, and report suspicious messages. |
| Email and URL filtering | Blocks or flags known phishing links and look-alike domains before users see them.

7. How defenders can detect it
Common warning signs in sign-in logs include:
- The same session used from two different IPs, countries, or devices within a short time (token replay).
- A successful sign-in followed shortly by a new MFA method registration or other security-info change.
- New inbox rules that forward, delete, or hide messages right after a sign-in.
- Sign-ins from unusual hosting providers or networks (ASNs), or with unfamiliar user agents.
- Risky sign-in alerts from the identity provider that don't match the user's normal behavior.
Detection queries for these are in the [`/detections`](../detections) folder of this repo.

